> the 'bot would select something to eat and then eat it (or even
> pray once it got down to that point).

Also rog-o-matic doesn't ‘learns to play', but uses knowledge rules
for it. In fact, if I remember well the article I read (I think the
same one that everyone has read) it uses a sort of ‘static rules',
which means that all of them has the same priority, and with that
basis then all the strategy of r-o-m must be a sort of ‘hard-wired' in
the system (or kind of)… If we consider that for NH the strategy is so
complex for almost every part of the game (and changing at any moment
for anything the RNG wants to add, like a hobbit with a wand of death)
then we can assume that if we use an expert system (like r-o-m) then
we must now use dynamic changing knowledge rules, with changing of
priorities and all of that… (that's also kind of ‘hard-wired', but you
know what I meant…)

I'm no expert in any of the approaches (expert systems or
‘evolutionary software'), but I think that using ‘evolutionary' means
also that the bot will have to, sort of, learn to play, not from the
beginning, but from the fact that it almost develop its strategies,
and learn from its mistakes, and I don't know what else… For the other
side, a pure expert system will may be (and just may…) kind of
precise, BUT (and a great one) it will be very very complex, or
better, large more than complex.

Even with that, I prefer the expert system approach (well, I really
liked more the hybrid, but if I should choose only one that would be).
Maybe for the same reasons that I am more used with fixed systems…

And thinking a little about that, I imagine the bot also like the one
proposed with repriorization of rules and all that… also, with a lot
of pre-knowledge of the game: the tactics for ID'ing (using maybe the
r-o-m ones), the fixed part of special levels, all the stats for the
monsters, the meaning of the messages that the game gives (discarding
maybe the rumors, because the bot will know that they are true or
false and since it is not learning or building strategies, then they
are almost useless (except for the stats-training maybe), and here
maybe (I'm supposing a lot here) also the Oracle will be pointless for
it, except for the fact that it also is a monster that may be killed
for experiences or items, but also there is the alignment hurt to
consider…), and also things that maybe a human player doesn't uses
because of the mentioned ‘boredom' fact, like to consider an
approximation for the status of the counters in the game (those that
tell you when it is safe to pray, or when your luck resets, etc.) In
this case maybe the bot won't know exactly when will it be safe to
pray again, but it will approximately know when it will… by
maintaining the most probable, and the minimum and maximum values that
those counters may take (which I think will never be the same, for the
factors that the good RNG always adds…) but they maybe useful… (Even
this approach may seem exagerated, to know almost everything and
practically confront the bot only with the RNG (which I think is a
very nice challenge), but maybe we can consider that, since it is
called expert system, it must be the most near to an hypothetic expert
player, which knows all of this information and uses to its benefit.
Ok, that maybe cheating for a human player, but what if it ‘learns'
this information by pure experience and then uses it in its games?
Then the expert system bot will ‘skip' the learning process and only
use the experience learned, lets say the ideal experience a most
experienced player should have.)

But, we have the other important fact (the big BUT), the same RNG may
change the things so much that a given strategy, even with
repriorization of the rules will be poor, or at least it must be very
very too large to work well…

How about if we, sort of, classify different situations in the game?
Lets say that we consider a general strategy for some parts of the
game (maybe the most, maybe not), but also for other parts we have to
consider the role-race-alignment-gender combination of the character
(and even maybe I think that even if it's different to play a
barbarian than a wizard, a human and an orcish barbarian will have a
lot of common things in a lot of parts of the strategy to play… I
don't know it for sure… I am another one which never had conquered
even a single YAFAP…). And also, maybe some situations in the game may
be good candidates of classifying as kind of the same type, like…
well, I don't know, maybe all the hallucinations in the early game, at
the first… 5 levels? And some monsters appearing… may be treated the
same way… with some exceptions of course, and the more general
division that gender-race-aling-gender gives… (and the common points
also), but then we have a thing to start… if the classification is
possible, then the situations may be reduced in number, and different
strategies, with repriorizable rules all of them, would be more
manageable.

Of course, I don't mean to classify EVERY situation in the game (the
RNG gives so much to think playing with all those properties, monsters
and items in the game…) but if some generalization can be reached,
then maybe the resulting bot will play, not excellent, but at least
good, at least in those games which don't give the unclassified
situations, and even then maybe the strategies could try to ‘take the
risk' and confront that unexpected monster with strange items, or try
to run away, I don't know… And with a little luck it may win also in
those situations…. Besides, luck also plays some factor in every game,
even from the most experienced ones, is not? And when more research is
done, maybe the bot can be improved and it may improve its winning
ratio…

And maybe also, the bot could be a simple one, I mean that it will try
to finish the game (ascend) in the standard way, by now not trying to
follow any conduct (which anyway are optional), that would be a very
great goal to reach… then when it's improved, then we can think about
conducts, and trying to win in a lesser number of turns, or getting a
maximum punctuation…. I don't know…

Also it can then be implemented ‘evolutionary' algorithms and the
strategies developed there could be analyzed not only from the ‘expert
player' point of view, but also as a way of improving the strategies
that even the experts may use…

I'm just imagining, but that thing is good also…

